{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1793590000000-engineer-module-pins-r2b.prompt.md; cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-17 08:00:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-17 15:00:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: pending (provider-generated)\n# Task: module-pins COMMIT 2: module and host-export pins in `requires`, lower re-discharges by equal address and profile, holder driver seam, fixture re-mint (Architect steps 8-16), uncommitted\nRole: VM Runtime\nImplementers:\n- Model: gpt-6.1-sol | Assigned: 2026-10-17 15:00 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: continues the slice; commit 1 (R4) is merged to master as ce76c846; Architect ruled Part B and gave exact steps\n\nWorktree /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-pins (branch srvm-module-pins; HEAD 62121c7e = commit 1, ALREADY merged to origin/master ce76c846; tree clean). Read COMPLETELY ./collab/*architect-module-pins-review.report.md: Part B (ruling B.1 items 1-6: host modules DO participate; the lift records only used exports; two maps in `requires`: `:yin.k/modules {symbol -> manifest-address-string-or-nil}` and `:yin.k/host-exports {qualified-symbol -> profile-keyword}`; the lower's `check-modules!` with terminal `:module-pin-mismatch` / `:host-export-mismatch` and one collected `:yin.k/unsatisfied` with `:yin.k/discovery :complete` and `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/modules {...} :yin.k/host-exports {...}}`, zero attaches) and Part C steps 8-16 and the threat model ./collab/*architect-fetch-threat-model.report.md sections 3, 4.2, 4.4, 4.5, 5, 7. EXECUTE steps 8-16 EXACTLY (lift collector in/near discharge!, encoder/decoder/validator + malformed-slot collection extending `:requires`, `check-modules!` replacing `check-install-admission!` (absorbing it: carried install children listed in `:yin.k/admitted-modules` fold into the same collected shape), driver seam forwarding `(:yin.k/admitted-modules (:config state))` from `driver/lowered` and the fenced recovery call plus one driver test with and without the config, tests T1-T5, T8-T10 as amended in the ruling, the five mutants of step 13, fixture re-mint with old -> new addresses in the report and a completed dated I-8 entry in docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md, the doc amendments of step 15 including closing the known-gaps rows (Q5, R4 landed, P2-1 driver seam closed) and fixing the stale Part-B statements Architect P3-2 listed (known-gaps Q5 \"blocked\" row, continuation Q5 \"report specifies no carrier slot\", UCF \"blocked pending the host-export-profile slot/schema\" text), tidy the lower-task docstring as P3-4). Also drop the trivially true assertion at rows_test ~:1396 (P3-5) and align the lift's strip with the lower's refusal predicate if trivial (P3-1: strip every keyword key in the yin.admit namespace). The fetch LOOP is NOT part of this slice (no fetch/clock/lease code in rows.cljc or the lower; T9 pins layering). RULES: no src/yang, no test/yang, no collab commits, never --yolo, no commit/stage; if any pinned A address, descriptor hash or golden OTHER than the rows fixtures carrying `requires` moves, STOP and report old/new; if the ruling is ambiguous (e.g. how an entry's `:address` is read for a module resolved through the family, what `requires` emits for empty maps), STOP that sub-part and report; never weaken an assertion. Verification foreground: per-iteration `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the focused namespaces (rows-test, rows-custody-test, rows-checkpoint-test, remote-test, holder.*, safepoint-harness-test, frame-test, layout-test, stage-e-rows-test, linker-test, linker-manifest-test, mint-test) + `-r '^dao\\.jing\\..*'`; kondo on changed files (0 new); `git diff --check`; the whole JVM quick tier unrestricted at the end. No Node/Dart (orchestrator). Report ./collab/<ms>-engineer-module-pins-r2b.report.md with headers, per-step results, counts, fixtures old/new, mutants with named killers, what you STOPPED on, proposed commit split (code / tests+fixture / docs; driver seam separable?), truthful. End READY or BLOCKED.\n---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/*architect-module-pins-review.report.md collab/*architect-fetch-threat-model.report.md docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git status --short; rg --files -g AGENTS.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/*architect-module-pins-review.report.md collab/*architect-fetch-threat-model.report.md docs/agents/build-n-test.md; git status --short; rg --files -g AGENTS.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-16 06:06:47 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-16 13:06:47 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1)\nSession-ID: pending\nRole: Architect (read-only; worktree datomworld-srvm-pins, branch srvm-module-pins @ 6df441d0, 13 uncommitted files)\nBrief: collab/1793540000000-architect-module-pins-review.prompt.md\nInputs: collab/1792130530811-engineer-module-pins.report.md, collab/1793520000000-architect-fetch-threat-model.report.md (§4.2, §4.4, §6), the full `git diff`, and the sources cited below.\n\n# Module pins: Part A review of R4, Part B ruling on host-module pins, Part C round-2 steps\n\n## Part A: review of the uncommitted R4 part\n\n### A.0 What was checked\n\n- Gate placement: `check-install-admission!` (`rows.cljc:1883-1892`) runs in `lower-task` after `check-custody!` and before `attach-streams!` (`rows.cljc:2301-2304`), over every task of `task-tree` (`rows.cljc:1805-1808`, root and every nested install child, pre-order). Refusal throws before the first attach: the attach-count law holds (tests assert zero attaches).\n- Paths that lower install children: only `rows/lower-task`. Backends (`layout/lower-task`) refuse `:installs` already; `remote.cljc:485` lowers no installs; fork packs go through the same `lower-task`; `holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` now forwards `:yin.k/admitted` (`export.cljc:539`); the holder driver's two lower paths are discussed in P2-1.\n- Other copies of the sender's admission: `engine/link-install` (`engine.cljc:1436-1443`) is the only consumer of the four `:yin.admit/*` response fields outside the linker; nothing else reads the installed entry's `:admission` (grep over `src`: only `linker/admission.cljc`, `linker/interpreter.cljc`, `yin.repl` configuration, none read `(:admission entry)`). Dropping rather than replacing is safe for consumers.\n- Refusal shape: `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied :yin.k/missing {:yin.k/modules {name carried-address}}}` matches threat-model §4.4 exactly (no `:yin.k/kind`, no `:yin.k/discovery`). The carried address can never be nil at this point because `check-install!` already required a manifest map (`rows.cljc:1846-1851`), so `{'host.mod nil}` in the admitted map correctly refuses.\n- Mutants: all three are meaningful (name-only acceptance, retained sender admission, gate bypass) and each is killed by distinct assertions. Mutant 2 has to be re-done after P1-1 (see below).\n- Tests: portable (plain data, keyword sentinel addresses, host-neutral catch forms, kondo clean per the engineer). Fixtures admit their fixed manifests explicitly from the composition side (`rows_support.cljc:298-301`, `safepoint_harness_test.cljc:349-352`), never derived from the pack.\n- Docs: continuation §2.5 and §7.1 (\"the lower does not re-discharge free names\") are unchanged and still true, since the pin work has not landed. UCF §7.6.4/§7.6.5 are unchanged spec text. The §7.11 row, continuation Q5, revisions I-8 and the known-gaps rows say \"blocked\" for the pin carrier: no overclaim. The claims that are wrong after P1-1 are listed under P3-3.\n\n### A.1 Findings\n\n**P1-1. Dropping the sender's admission at restore breaks fenced recovery and the relift law for every real pack with an install child.**\nThe lift copies the install record's `response` whole into the `:install` row (`install-node`, `rows.cljc:1643`). The linker's interpreter puts `:yin.admit/id`, `:yin.admit/decision`, `:yin.admit/asserters` and `:yin.admit/address` on every successful DHT-admitted response (`interpreter.cljc:416-421`), so a real pack's `:install` row carries them. The new restore-side `dissoc` (`rows.cljc:2236-2237`) then produces a machine whose `:installs` response lacks the four keys. Consequences:\n- `rehydrate-fenced` re-prepares and re-encodes the restored machine and demands byte equality with the published pack (`export.cljc:548-556`); the relift's install row now differs from the carried one, so every fenced recovery of such a pack answers `:yin.k/undecodable :recovery-inconsistent`.\n- The harness law \"the relift equals the lift by root\" (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:10`, asserted at `:629` via `canonical-under`) fails for the same packs.\nThe current tests pass only because every fixture response (`rows_support/module-response`, `blocking-body`) carries no `:yin.admit/*` keys. This is a correctness defect, not a test gap.\nRequired fix (revert-not-fix does not apply, this is the slice's own line): strip the four `:yin.admit/*` keys **at lift**, in `install-node`, so the pack never carries S's decision (\"the carrier narrows, never widens\", §4.1), and make the lower **refuse** a carried response that still carries any `:yin.admit/*` key as `:yin.k/undecodable :install-response` (one clause in `check-install!`'s existing `and`; hand-crafted packs only). Delete the restore-side `dissoc`. Then the restored response equals the carried one, fenced reproduction and the relift law hold by construction, and `link-install` sees no sender record. New pin for the mutant: a source whose install response carries the record lifts to an `:install` row without the keys (assert on the decoded row's slot 3), and a pack with the keys spliced back in is refused before attach with zero attaches. Keep the existing \"installed entry has no `:admission`\" assertion.\n\n**P2-1. The holder driver has no seam to supply `:yin.k/admitted`, so the composition's real lower path refuses every pack with an install child.**\n`driver/lowered` is called with `{:exclusive true}` (`driver.cljc:2294`) and with the grant map (`driver.cljc:1588-1600`); the fenced recovery at `driver.cljc:2455-2457` passes only `:address` and `:attach`. None forwards an admitted map. This is fail-closed (safe) but it means R4 as written makes carried install children unreachable through the holder, with no driver test noticing (`driver_test.cljc` has no install-child handoff; `:2726` fakes one for gate-mode only). Required for round 2, not for landing R4 alone: a config entry `(:yin.k/admitted-modules (:config state))` forwarded by `lowered` and by the fenced recovery call (two lines), plus one driver test that lowers a parked-installer checkpoint with and without the config. Until then, a known-gaps row \"holder driver passes no admitted-modules map; install-child handoff through the driver refuses\" (P2) must land with R4.\n\n**P2-2. `:yin.k/admitted` already means two other things in the same vocabulary.**\nIt is a ledger fact kind, \"one effect's dedup record with its result\" (`ledger.cljc:22, 115, 553`), and the driver's not-holder answer field for the admitted variant set (`driver.cljc:743, 1655`). The threat model (§4.4, my text) chose the word carelessly. Rename the lower option to `:yin.k/admitted-modules` in `rows.cljc`, `export.cljc`, the five test files and the five doc amendments. Cheap now, a permanent vocabulary defect after commit.\n\n**P3-1. The gate reports one child, not the complete set, and omits `:yin.k/discovery`.**\nEvery other `:yin.k/unsatisfied` from the lower carries `:yin.k/discovery` (`rows.cljc:1629, 1686, 1923, 1930`). §4.4's shape omitted it; §4.2's has `:complete`. Ruling: in round 2 fold the admission gate into the single `check-modules!` stage (Part C step 5), which collects every unlisted child of the tree and every unsatisfied pin into one `:yin.k/missing` map with `:yin.k/discovery :complete`. Not blocking for landing R4 alone; the §4.4 shape is acceptable until then.\n\n**P3-2. Name-keyed admission across the tree.** The admitted map is flat by module name over the whole tree. A nested child that installs the same name as a sibling at another address cannot be admitted. The registry family makes this a degenerate case; note it in the `lower-task` docstring, no code.\n\n**P3-3. Doc statements to retarget after P1-1.** Continuation §7.2 amendment (\"Restore drops the sender's …\"), Q3 amendment (\"drops the sender's admission evidence on restore\"), `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:2333-2335`, transport-tuples amendment (\"Restore drops the sender's four …\"), revisions I-8 (\"restore drops\"), known-gaps R4 row (\"sender admission dropped on restore\"). All become \"the lift never carries the sender's admission fields; a carried one is `:yin.k/undecodable :install-response`\". Also replace `:yin.k/admitted` by the P2-2 name in all five docs.\n\n**P3-4. `holder.export/rehydrate-fenced` docstring** says \"default empty\" twice in different words; fine. No change needed.\n\nVerdict for Part A: **CHANGES-REQUIRED** (P1-1 blocking; P2-2 rename and the P2-1 ledger row land with it; P3-1/P3-2 deferred to round 2).\n\n## Part B: ruling on host-module (\"nil\") pins\n\n### B.0 Facts the ruling rests on\n\n- A host-registered entry has `:address nil` and lists its exports as `:yin.module/primitives {sym profile-address}` (`register-host-module`, `module.cljc:223-263`). `module/host-export-profile` answers exactly that profile address for a qualified symbol (`module.cljc:266-281`): a **keyword** `:yin.k.pp/blake3-…` minted by `vm/primitive-profile` over `{name class arities effects host-state}` (`vm.cljc:338-356`). Plain data, no functions, no host objects. The core `stream` and `cell` modules are host modules with literal cljc profiles and `:none` host-state (`module.cljc:350-363, 405-410`), so their addresses are identical on JVM, Node and Dart.\n- The linker already defines \"satisfied by equal profile\" for exactly this case: `declared-discharge` discharges a primitive obligation by `(= profile (module/host-export-profile modules name))` (`linker.cljc:1014-1018`), and a module obligation by equal manifest address (`:1022-1027`). UCF §7.6.5 already carries `:yin.k/primitives {name <expected profile>}` in the outcome.\n- The lift's `discharge!` answers a qualified free name by registry presence (`rows.cljc:1611-1616`) and nothing of the entry travels. The task row's `requires` slot is a plain map (`rows.cljc:1687-1689`), so two more keys cost no slot change.\n\n### B.1 Ruling\n\n1. **Host modules participate in this slice.** Not deferred. Reason: `stream` and `cell`, the most common modules in any pack, are host modules; deferring leaves R6 (silent skew) open for the common case while closing it only for DHT-linked modules. The data is already pure, the comparison already exists in the linker, and the lift already resolves the entry. The risk of implementing is one more `requires` key and a fixture re-mint, both owed anyway for `:yin.k/modules`. The risk of deferring is a second re-mint later and a documented hole in the fetch-loop contract.\n\n2. **What the lift records.** Only what the live code uses: for every live free name `ns/f` that `discharge!` answered through the registry (`rows.cljc:1614-1616`, after `E`, store, primitives and installing names, which stay as they are):\n   - `:yin.k/modules` gets `{ns <manifest address>}` when the entry is linked (`(:address entry)` non-nil), `{ns nil}` when it is host-registered;\n   - `:yin.k/host-exports` gets `{ns/f <profile address>}` when the entry is host-registered, the value being `(module/host-export-profile (:modules m) 'ns/f)`.\n   Not all exports. Recording all exports would refuse a receiver whose host module gained or changed an export the code never touches, and it would persist what the free-name scan already derives. A lift where `host-export-profile` answers nil for a name the registry claims to hold (an entry with a malformed `:yin.module/primitives`) is `:yin.k/non-portable :host-export-profile {:yin.k/name ns/f}`.\n\n3. **Canonical encoding.** Two maps in the task row's `requires` beside `:yin.k/segments`, `:yin.k/trees` and `:yin.k/cursor-profiles`: `:yin.k/modules {symbol -> string-or-nil}` and `:yin.k/host-exports {qualified-symbol -> keyword}`. Keys sorted by the carrier's existing rules for symbol keys; the `keyed`/`built` host-collapse discipline applies (module names contain dots, no slash; a qualified export has one slash, so the known P3 `keyed` gap does not reach these maps, but add the count guard anyway as the `built` precedent). Absent maps are not emitted when empty (same as `:yin.k/trees` with no segments is still emitted; follow whatever the existing `requires` does for empties, byte-stable). No richer value in `:yin.k/modules`: a tagged vector `[:host profiles]` beside address strings mixes value kinds in one slot and duplicates the primitives precedent for no gain.\n\n4. **How the lower compares** (new `check-modules!`, one pass over the whole tree, after `check-custody!`, before `attach-streams!`, absorbing today's `check-install-admission!`):\n   - `:yin.k/modules {ns address}`: receiver's `module/resolve-module` entry exists with `(= address (:address entry))` → satisfied. Entry exists with another address (including a host entry, nil, where a linked one was pinned, or vice versa) → **terminal** `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied :yin.k/kind :module-pin-mismatch :yin.k/name ns :yin.k/pinned address :yin.k/linked (:address entry)}`, the `:dependency-pin-mismatch` words (`engine.cljc:1388-1393`). Entry absent → collected into `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/modules {ns address}}`.\n   - `:yin.k/host-exports {ns/f profile}`: `(= profile (module/host-export-profile (:modules recv) 'ns/f))` → satisfied. Receiver answers another profile → **terminal** `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied :yin.k/kind :host-export-mismatch :yin.k/name ns/f :yin.k/pinned profile :yin.k/held <receiver's>}`. Receiver answers nil (no such export, or the module is absent, or it is linked rather than host) → collected into `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/host-exports {ns/f profile}}` (the `:yin.k/modules {ns nil}` entry will be in the same map when the module is absent).\n   - Receiver's host module exporting **more** than the pin names: satisfied (subset by construction, since only used exports are pinned). Equality is of profile addresses, never of normalised profile maps: the address already covers name, class, arities, effects and host-state.\n   - Carried install children: `:yin.k/admitted-modules {ns address}` equality as today; unlisted → collected into `:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/modules {ns carried-address}}`.\n   - Order: terminal mismatches are thrown first (nothing the composition can fetch repairs them), then one collected refusal `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied :yin.k/discovery :complete :yin.k/missing {…}}` with keys present only where something is missing. Zero attaches in every case.\n   - The fetch loop (composition, not this slice) requires only keys of `:yin.k/missing :yin.k/modules` whose pin is non-nil; a nil pin and every `:yin.k/host-exports` entry are composition prerequisites and are not fetched.\n\n5. **Determinism across hosts.** Profile addresses are BLAKE3 content hashes over plain data computed identically on all three hosts (the same mechanism Stage E already pins for primitives); manifest addresses are segment keys. The refusal maps are plain data. Pin T8 in `stage_e_rows_test.cljc`: a `requires` with both maps re-encodes byte-identically and the two terminal refusals and the collected refusal are `=` across JVM, Node and Dart.\n\n6. **Threat-model text amended by this ruling.** §4.2's shape gains `:yin.k/host-exports`; \"whose export profiles equal those the lift recorded\" becomes \"whose profile address for each used export equals the pinned one\"; §4.4's option is renamed `:yin.k/admitted-modules` and its refusal is folded into the collected shape with `:yin.k/discovery :complete`. T4 reads: a nil pin plus `:yin.k/host-exports` is satisfied by a host module answering equal profile addresses for the pinned exports; another profile for a pinned export refuses `:host-export-mismatch`; a missing export is collected as missing; extra exports are ignored.\n\n### B.2 Alternative considered and rejected\n\n\"Refuse nothing for nil pins, document, defer\": `:yin.k/modules {ns nil}` would then only assert \"a host module of this name must exist\", which `resolve-var` already enforces at run time, and skew in `stream`/`cell` profiles between two compositions would still run under R's authority (R6, R8 for the common case). The saving is one map and one lift line; not worth a second re-mint. Rejected.\n\n## Part C: ordered steps for codex round 2\n\nPreconditions: worktree `datomworld-srvm-pins`, the current 13 files uncommitted. `npm ci` and `mise trust` are done there. Per-iteration verification is `bb test:clj`; full three-host `bb test` once at the end, one lane set at a time, foreground only. Read `docs/agents/build-n-test.md` first. No `collab/` path is ever staged.\n\n**Commit 1 (R4, lands alone): fix P1-1, rename P2-2, ledger P2-1.**\n1. `rows.cljc` `install-node`: `response` → `(dissoc response :yin.admit/id :yin.admit/decision :yin.admit/asserters :yin.admit/address)` before the `cbor/encode` check and the row. Delete the restore-side `dissoc` (`rows.cljc:2233-2237` and its comment).\n2. `rows.cljc` `check-install!`: add to the existing `and` of the `:install-response` clause `(not-any? #(= \"yin.admit\" (namespace %)) (filter keyword? (keys response)))`.\n3. Rename `:yin.k/admitted` → `:yin.k/admitted-modules` in `rows.cljc`, `export.cljc`, `rows_support.cljc`, `rows_test.cljc`, `rows_custody_test.cljc`, `export_test.cljc`, `safepoint_harness_test.cljc`.\n4. Tests in `rows_test.cljc` `carried-install-requires-receiver-admission`: keep every assertion; add (a) the decoded wire's root `:install` row slot 3 has none of the four keys, (b) the harness-style relift of the lowered machine is `=` by root to the wire (reuse `canonical-under` or compare the install row), (c) a pack with the four keys spliced into the install row refuses `:yin.k/undecodable :install-response` with zero attaches. In `export_test.cljc`: a frozen parked-installer whose source response carried the sender record rehydrates `:ok` and reproduces the pack byte for byte (this is the P1-1 regression pin).\n5. Mutants (run, record counts, restore): (m1) lift keeps the sender keys → 4(a), 4(b)/export pin fail; (m2) `check-install!` accepts `:yin.admit/*` keys → 4(c) fails; (m3) gate bypass → as before.\n6. Docs (P3-3 list): reword \"dropped on restore\" to \"never carried by the lift; a carried one is refused\", rename the option, add the known-gaps row for P2-1 (holder driver seam, P2) and the P3-2 docstring note. Revisions I-8 wording likewise.\n7. `bb test:clj`, kondo on changed files, `git diff --check`. Report; Architect sign-off; commit `fix(yin.vm.ucf): carried install children need receiver admission; lift never carries sender admission` (format.md convention, no Co-Authored-By).\n\n**Commit 2 (pins carrier + lift + lower + driver seam): the ruling.**\n8. `rows.cljc` lift: in `discharge!` (or a sibling collector over the same `answered?` walk), record for every live registry-answered name the entry: linked → `:yin.k/modules {ns address}`; host → `:yin.k/modules {ns nil}` and `:yin.k/host-exports {ns/f profile}`; nil profile → `:yin.k/non-portable :host-export-profile`. Put both maps into `requires` (`rows.cljc:1687-1689`), sorted, empty maps handled as the existing `requires` keys handle empties.\n9. Encoder/decoder/validator: `requires` is a plain map today, so confirm `check-dependencies!` (`rows.cljc:1918`) still accepts the two extra keys and add the key-count guard for symbol keys as in `built`. Malformed-slot collection: non-map `:yin.k/modules`, non-symbol key, non-string-non-nil value, non-keyword `:yin.k/host-exports` value, unqualified `:yin.k/host-exports` key → `:yin.k/undecodable :requires` (extend the existing kind, no new kind).\n10. `rows.cljc` lower: replace `check-install-admission!` by `check-modules!` per B.1 item 4 (terminal mismatches first, then one collected refusal with `:yin.k/discovery :complete`, zero attaches), same position (after custody, before attach). Delete `check-install-admission!`.\n11. Driver seam (P2-1): `driver/lowered` and the fenced recovery call forward `(:yin.k/admitted-modules (:config state))`; `rehydrate-fenced` already forwards. One driver test with and without the config over a parked-installer checkpoint.\n12. Tests (threat model §7.1, amended): T1 (lift of harness row 17 emits `{:yin.k/modules {host.mod nil} :yin.k/host-exports {host.mod/f <profile>}}`; a DHT-linked fixture emits `{pym.k <address>}`; names answered by `E`, store, prims or module stores emit nothing), T2 (receiver lacking the module: collected refusal, `:discovery :complete`, zero attaches), T3 (linked module at another address: `:module-pin-mismatch` naming both; equal address lowers), T4 (B.1 item 6), T5 (already in commit 1, re-point to the collected shape), T8 (Stage E cross-host row), T9 (layering: `rows.cljc` requires neither `linker.interpreter`, `linker.dht` nor `dao.lease`), T10 (`resolve-var` unchanged). T6/T7 (fetch loop, lease) are composition work, out of this slice; leave their known-gaps rows.\n13. Mutants (run, record, restore): lift records all exports instead of used; lower compares by name presence instead of address; lower accepts a different profile address; lower reports `:incomplete`; attach before `check-modules!`. Each must be killed by a named test.\n14. Fixture re-mint: `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` and any ledger/Stage E fixture whose `requires` now carries the two maps; record old → new addresses in the report and in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` (I-8 completes, dated). C4 scanner pins are untouched (AST-level; see the standing note).\n15. Docs: continuation §2.5 (binding fact travels: the two maps), §7.1 step 6 (records address or host profile), §7.2 dependencies row (module check, three refusal kinds, delete \"the lower does not re-discharge\"), §11 Q5 closed as C with the host-export ruling, Q3 (admitted set is `:yin.k/admitted-modules`); UCF §7.6.4 (add the host-export sentence), §7.6.5 (add `:yin.k/host-exports` to the outcome), §7.11 row updated; transport grammar (`requires` slot: the two maps); `yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 7.5.11 amendment reworded; threat-model report is a collab artifact, not edited; known-gaps: Q5 row → landed, R4 row → landed, P2-1 row → closed by step 11.\n16. `bb test:clj` per iteration; at the end `bb test` all three hosts (foreground, one lane set at a time; \"Testing <ns>\" must appear in Node output); kondo; `git diff --check`. Report with counts, mutant kills, old → new addresses. Architect sign-off, then commit `feat(yin.vm.ucf): module pins and host-export pins in requires; lower re-discharges by equal address and profile` and, if the driver seam is separable, a third commit `feat(yin.vm.ucf.holder): admitted-modules config forwarded to the lower`.\n\nCommit split summary: (1) R4 alone, after the P1-1 fix and the rename; (2) carrier + lift + lower + tests + re-mint + docs; (3) optional driver seam if it would otherwise delay (2).\n\nCHANGES-REQUIRED (Part A)\nRULING-READY\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-17 03:00:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-17 10:00:00 +07\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-16 05:10:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-16 12:10:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1)\nSession-ID: pending\nRole: Architect (read-only; worktree datomworld-threat at 6df441d0 = origin/master)\nBrief: collab/1793520000000-architect-fetch-threat-model.prompt.md\n\n# Threat model: policy-gated receiver-side fetch of modules a lowered continuation names (continuation §11 Q5)\n\n## 0. One-paragraph answer\n\nUsing the linker from the lower *can* enable injection, but not through the\nlinker's own pipeline: the pipeline verifies address, identity, derivation,\nmanifest name, declared obligations and (on the DHT path) asks the holder's\nadmission policy before any load. The injection routes are around it:\n(i) **name-only satisfaction** (today's `discharge!` and `resolve-var` match a\nmodule by *name*, so a same-named module of other content at R silently\nanswers; a fetch loop that requires by name inherits this), (ii) **carried\ninstall children** (a pack's `:install` rows are verified self-consistent and\nthen installed into R's registry family by `link-install` with S's admission\nrecord copied, never R's policy asked), and (iii) **a composition that seeds\nits name environment or `:trusted` derivation policy from carrier data**.\nOption D (fetch inside symbol resolution) is correctly rejected. The verdict is\n**C in UCF §7.6.4's existing form** (`:yin.k/modules {name manifest-address}`\nin `requires`), with B's mechanism (re-discharge on lower, refuse before\nattach, composition drives an explicit `require`, re-lower from the same\npack) and the install-child admission gap closed in the same slice.\n\n## 1. Assets and trust boundaries\n\n### 1.1 What R protects\n\n| Asset | Where it lives | Why it matters for a fetch loop |\n|---|---|---|\n| Module registry | `(:modules state)`, a value (`module.cljc:27`, linker I7 `yin.vm.linker.md:201`) | Exposure domain is the **installation family for its life**; a linked name answers later `require`s with no link request and no admission question (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md:2317-2333`); untrust is not retroactive (`:2335-2338`). Poisoning is sticky. |\n| Program store | isolated per task; the pack's slice replaces it (UCF §7.6.2 `:1623-1640`); pinned by `rows_test.cljc:1170-1179` | Not reachable from a pack: receiver-only store/module-store state is discarded, the carried slice never merges. |\n| Module stores | `:module-stores`, instantiated per task from the pack (`rows.cljc:2210-2213`) | Sender-chosen content; a closure with `store-of m` resolves names there **before** the registry (`engine.cljc:127-139`). Not a linker question: the pack carries code by design. |\n| Streams | attached only by the composition's `attach!`; declined = `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:dao.stream/identity}` (`rows.cljc:2052-2061`) | A pack names identities, it cannot mint handles. |\n| Authority and custody | grant, epoch, occurrence (UCF §7.7; `check-custody!` `rows.cljc:2008`) | A fetch that runs **after** the grant holds custody while it waits. |\n| Linker budgets | `default-bounds` `{:max-parts 65536 :max-depth 256 :max-bytes 16MB}` (`linker.cljc:1321`); `fetch` has no deadline (`:1958-1960`) | Amplification and starvation surface. |\n| Admission media | capacity 1024, evict-oldest (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md:2158-2163`) | Unanswered questions park links forever (`:2076-2086`). |\n| Privacy | DHT `get`s for manifests and closures | The names a pack makes R fetch are visible to DHT peers. |\n\n### 1.2 Who is trusted, and for what\n\n| Party | Trusted for | Not trusted for |\n|---|---|---|\n| S (sender) | Nothing by default. Under exclusive custody, the grantor's record binds `:checkpoint` to the pack address (UCF §7.7, `rows.cljc:1928-1959`). A fork pack is unbound data. | Which code R should install; which names exist; which addresses they resolve to. |\n| The carrier stream | Delivery and, when attached with an identity, attribution. Row ids and the pack address are BLAKE3 and re-verified at decode (continuation §7.2 `:1744`). | Authorship: the pack is not signed by S; integrity binds only to an address R must learn out of band (the grant) or not at all (fork). |\n| The DHT | Integrity of content-addressed bytes: `:address-mismatch`/`:hash-mismatch` (`linker.cljc:1392-1394`, ingress `:1202-1221`). | Availability, naming, authority. A tuple in an index \"never establishes its own authority\" (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md:1748-1750`). |\n| A module publisher | Only if **declared** (`--dht-principal`) does its assertion enter the fold; only if **trusted** does admission allow it (`:2292-2300`). Safety checks (L-e: profiles, pins, export origin) hold regardless (`:2342-2349`). | Anything when undeclared; code semantics even when trusted. |\n| R's composition | Root of trust: `:authority`, `:admission` policy, `attach!`, the drive, the lease judge. | It must not import trust from the pack (names, addresses, derivation policy). |\n\n## 2. Attacker models\n\n| Id | Attacker | Capabilities | Cannot |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Malicious sender | Chooses every row: code vectors, free names, module stores, install children with manifests/derivations/images, pins (option C), custody slots. | Forge a pack address a grant names; forge sealed resource refs (`:forged-resource-reference`, continuation §7.1 step 4); write R's ambient store. |\n| M2 | Honest sender, hostile network | Replace or mutate a pack in flight; replay an old pack; delay. | Pass decode with a mutated row (digest refusal); pass custody for an exclusive root without the grant evidence. |\n| M3 | Hostile DHT peer | Serve wrong or oversized content; withhold; serve slowly; serve a malformed closure. | Pass step 2/3 (address, identity); exceed `default-bounds`. |\n| M4 | Hostile module publisher (declared principal) | Assert any name it likes under its key, including names another publisher uses; republish; equivocate. | Enter the fold undeclared; win an ambiguous name (`:ambiguous-name`, `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:1789-1791`); survive equivocation (`yin.vm.linker.md:2010-2018`); be admitted when untrusted. |\n| M5 | Local malicious task | `require` any name; `try-require`; loop. | Write the registry except through install; reach `:resources` or the admission response medium; issue references without the task secret (`engine.cljc:1296-1301`). |\n\n## 3. Attack routes against options A-D\n\nOptions: **A** accept (composition prerequisite, today). **B** re-discharge on lower, refuse before attach with `:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/missing {:obligations …}}`, composition drives `require`, retries. **C** B plus the carrier pins the manifest address each registry-answered name resolved to at S; R satisfies only by equal address. **D** implicit fetch inside `resolve-var`.\n\nCode facts the table relies on (all read):\n- Lift `discharge!` answers a namespaced name by **presence** in S's registry, `(some? (module/resolve-module …))` (`rows.cljc:1615-1617`); nothing of the entry travels (continuation §2.5 `:360-366`).\n- Lower `check-dependencies!` checks segments, trees, store census, `attaches?`, closure origin/binders, `:prim` presence (`rows.cljc:1898-1925`); **no registry check** (continuation §7.2 `:1827-1830`). `restore-task` keeps `recv`'s `:modules` (`rows.cljc:2095-2099`; pinned at `rows_test.cljc:1168-1169`).\n- Runtime `resolve-var` resolves by name through the registry (`engine.cljc:133-139`), else `Unable to resolve symbol` (`:140`); called from the semantic evaluator with `(:modules machine)` (`semantic.cljc:152`).\n- The link-pair interpreter serves **by-name requests only** (`interpreter.cljc:483-486`), resolves through R's fold, asks admission after resolve and before load (`:437-462`), loads the **retained** address of the allow (`:412-413`), and `settle` re-checks the manifest's name (`engine.cljc:1540-1548`) and runs 5b against live state (`:1214-1237`).\n\n| # | Route | Precondition | Impact | Mitigated today? (cite) | Residual under A | under B | under C | under D |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | **Name squatting / dependency confusion at R** (M4, M1): R holds or resolves `pym.k` to content other than S's. | A same-named module in R's registry, or a declared+trusted principal asserting the name. | Continuation runs against other code; silent semantic substitution, with the pack's effects authorised under R's streams. | Partly: the fold refuses ambiguity and unauthenticated envelopes (`yin.vm.linker.md:2065-2078`); admission refuses untrusted asserters. **Not** mitigated for a name R already holds: `resolve-var` has no address check; the pin invariant covers only manifest-declared `:yin.module/requires` (`module.cljc:113-142`), never a continuation's free name. | **High**: skew undetected. | **High**: name-present passes the re-discharge. | **Closed**: equal-address check (as `declared-discharge` `:module`, `linker.cljc:1021-1027`; UCF §7.6.4 `:1699-1702`). | High, plus implicit. |\n| R2 | **Carrier-driven fetch** (M1): pack names free symbols chosen to make R fetch. | A composition fetch loop. | R issues DHT loads and admission questions for attacker-chosen names; cost, leak, parked links. | No loop exists today. The linker side bounds one fetch (`default-bounds`) and asks admission per name (`:2037-2049`). `:composition` provenance is **not asked** (`interpreter.cljc:447`). | None (no fetch). | **Medium**: loop runs by name; bounded by distinct names in the pack (decode budget) and by R's policy. | **Medium→Low**: a pin lets the loop refuse before fetching when R's own resolution disagrees (one fold, no load). | **High**: every `:var` may fetch; no explicit effect. |\n| R3 | **Identity substitution** (M3, M4): wrong bytes for an address, or a manifest whose derivation does not lead to its image. | Hostile content source. | Would install unverified code. | **Yes**: steps 2/3 (`linker.cljc:1392`), manifest name (`:2400-2404`), derivation `:verifying` (`:2164-2216`), `foreign-image` at install (`engine.cljc:1415-1422`), `check-install!` for carried children (`rows.cljc:1853-1858`). | Low | Low | Low | Low |\n| R4 | **Carried install child bypasses R's admission** (M1). | Pack carries an `:install` row (semantic kernel accepts; backends refuse `:installs`, continuation §7.2 `:1760`). | After the child halts at R, `link-install` receives the module into R's registry **family** with **S's** `:admission` record copied (`engine.cljc:1436-1443`); later `require`s answer from the registry, never asked. **Registry poisoning via the carrier.** | **No.** `check-install!` verifies self-consistency only; the design says binding to composition-known addresses \"remains the linker's fetch/admission responsibility\" (continuation §11 Q3 `:2744-2745`) and no one performs it at lower. Only the sealed child secret (`:secret-source`) and L-e checks apply. | **High** (exists today, independent of Q5) | High unless fixed | **Closed if** carried installs pass the same equal-address + R-admission rule (§4.4). | n/a |\n| R5 | **TOCTOU between check and fetch/install** (M5, race): registry changes between the refusal and the re-lower. | Another task links the same name meanwhile. | Re-lower sees name present (B) → skew; or install races a concurrent install (`:installs` join, `engine.cljc:1536`). | Lower is pure over a `recv` value and attaches nothing on refusal (attach-count law, continuation §7.2 `:1800-1803`). First link wins for module stores (`engine.cljc:1107-1108`). | n/a | **Medium**: name-present check is race-sensitive. | **Low**: equality against the pin is race-insensitive; the composition re-lowers from the same bytes. | High |\n| R6 | **Version skew, same name different code at S and R** (honest both). | Different snapshot sets or principals at S and R. | Wrong behaviour, not detectable at run time. | No. | High | High | **Closed** (refuse `:yin.k/module-pin-mismatch` naming both addresses). | High |\n| R7 | **Fetch amplification and cycles** (M1, M3). | Many names; deep closures; mutually requiring modules. | CPU, bytes, parked links. | Per-fetch `default-bounds`; `:parts-limit` (`linker.cljc:1349`); `:require-cycle` on install ancestry (`module.cljc:592-599`); transitive requires are the child's own requests. **No** bound on the number of requires a composition issues, **no** deadline (`fetch` `:1958`; link-policy `:lease` deferred, `yin.repl.link-policy.md:156-160`). | None | **Medium**: needs a per-run budget and lease (§4.3). | Medium, same | High |\n| R8 | **Side effect before failure** (A's runtime symptom). | A missing name reached after an effect. | Partial execution under R's authority; under exclusive custody the occurrence is consumed and a terminal/successor edge is owed (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md:4137-4157`). | No: A fails at `engine.cljc:140` at run time. | **High** | **Closed** (refuse before attach). | Closed | Closed only if the fetch succeeds; otherwise a mid-run park with network. |\n| R9 | **Information leak through fetch requests** (M3 observers). | Fetch loop over the DHT. | Peers learn which modules R resumes; admission medium learns names too (by design). | DHT `get` is inherent; `:manual` link policy lets the holder decline. | None | **Low-Medium**: composition policy decides; pins allow a local-only check first. | Low-Medium | High (unconsented) |\n| R10 | **Registry poisoning** (M1 via R4; M4 via trust). | See R4; or a trusted malicious principal. | Family-wide, lifetime. | For M4: admission is the designed control; L-e safety floor. For M1: **not mitigated** (R4). | High | High | Closed with R4 fix | n/a |\n| R11 | **Policy bypass of `:admit`/ShiBi** (composition error, M1-induced). | Loop satisfies a missing name through `:name-env` (provenance `:composition`, never asked, `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:2008`) or a non-DHT source. | Admission skipped for carrier-chosen names. | The seam is sound; the bypass is a composition choice. Nothing today forbids seeding `:name-env` from a pack. | n/a | **Medium**: must be forbidden in the loop contract (§4.2). | Medium, same rule; the pin never substitutes for admission. | High |\n| R12 | **Downgrade** (composition error). | Loop links under `:derivation :trusted` (`linker.cljc:2504-2519`) or against a `:fallback` format. | Derivation unverified for carrier-named modules. | `:trusted` is meant for a composition that is its own publisher (`yin.vm.linker.md:1096-1102`). | n/a | Medium | Medium | High |\n| R13 | **Replay / substitution of the pack itself** (M2). | Fork packs, or a receiver that does not check the grant's `:checkpoint`. | An old or attacker-built pack lowers. | Exclusive: grant names the pack address (`rows.cljc:1928-1959`). Fork: out of scope of this question; a fork is data the composition chose to run. | Same in all options; a fetch loop must bind its retry to **one** pack address (§4.3). |\n\n## 4. Required mechanisms for a safe policy-gated fetch loop\n\n### 4.1 Who decides\n\n- **The lower decides nothing and fetches nothing.** It reports, before any\n  attach, the complete set of unsatisfied module bindings. Pure, no stream, no\n  clock (as today for segments, trees, stores, prims).\n- **The receiving composition decides**, as holder context of R's registry and\n  owner of the link pair, the authority, the admission policy and the drive.\n  It may answer \"no\" (manual policy) and the pack stays refused.\n- **The carrier narrows, never widens.** A pin (C) can only make R refuse\n  what R would otherwise have accepted by name. It grants nothing: the fetch\n  still goes by **name** through R's fold and R's admission, and the installed\n  entry's address must then equal the pin.\n- **The VM never learns any of this** (I6 `yin.vm.linker.md:196-199`).\n\n### 4.2 The refusal outcome the lower must carry (exact shape)\n\n```clojure\n{:yin.k/status    :yin.k/unsatisfied\n :yin.k/discovery :complete                     ; the set below is exhaustive (one pass over all contexts)\n :yin.k/missing   {:yin.k/modules {pym.k :segment/blake3-…    ; C: the address S's registry held\n                                   host.mod nil}              ; a name S answered from a host module (no address)\n                   :obligations  #{pym.k/body host.mod/f}}}   ; the free names, as today\n```\n\n- Keys are plain data, sorted by the carrier's rules; no symbol-to-string\n  host collapse (one entry per module name).\n- `:yin.k/modules` values: the manifest address (`:address` of the registry\n  entry, `module.cljc:92`) for a linked module; `nil` for a host-registered\n  module (`register-host-module` entries carry no address). A `nil` pin is\n  satisfied by a host module of that name whose export profiles equal those\n  the lift recorded (reuse `module/host-export-profile`, `module.cljc:266`),\n  else refused. A composition that cannot supply the host module cannot\n  fetch it: host modules are composition prerequisites and stay so.\n- The composition drives the loop **without trusting names**: it requires\n  the key (a name) through its own link pair, the admission question carries\n  R's fold's address and asserters, and after `linked` it compares\n  `(:address entry)` with the pin. Mismatch is terminal:\n  `{:yin.k/status :yin.k/unsatisfied :yin.k/kind :module-pin-mismatch\n  :yin.k/name pym.k :yin.k/pinned … :yin.k/linked …}` (same words as\n  `:dependency-pin-mismatch`, `engine.cljc:1388-1393`).\n- Forbidden in the loop: adding `:name-env` entries from the pack; switching\n  `:derivation` to `:trusted`; passing `:fallback` formats the pack named;\n  requiring anything not in `:yin.k/modules`.\n\n### 4.3 Budgets, deadlines, atomicity\n\n- **Budget**: at most one `require` per key of `:yin.k/modules`, at most two\n  `lower-task` calls per pack address per attempt (refuse, then re-lower).\n  Transitive requirements are the install child's own (`yin.vm.linker.md:1788-1797`)\n  and stay inside the linker's existing bounds and cycle refusal.\n- **Deadline**: a `dao.lease` over the whole fetch run, subject\n  `[:yin.k/lower pack-address]` (per-run, link-policy.md §7 Q2), holder the\n  composition's drive, renewal on every round that made progress\n  (`round`'s `:progress?`, `interpreter.cljc:521-523`), judge composed in the\n  process that owns the link pair, reclaim = `abandon`/`retire` of every\n  link the run owns (`interpreter/retire` by run descriptor,\n  `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:2088-2129`) plus dropping the pack. `:cap` gives the\n  hard ceiling. No host clock in the loop; ticks are data (`dao.lease.md:104-112`).\n- **Order relative to custody**: fetch **before** proposing for the grant.\n  UCF 14.2.3 step 2 already puts \"requirements\" before the proposal\n  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md:4115-4119`). A stuck fetch therefore holds no\n  tenure, mints no occurrence and owes no successor. Only the final\n  re-lower runs under the grant, and it is pure and fast.\n- **Atomicity**: the lower is a pure function of `(recv, wire)`; the\n  composition passes the **same wire bytes** and a `recv` whose registry is\n  the value it observed after the installs. Because satisfaction is by equal\n  address, a concurrent change to the registry cannot turn a refusal into a\n  wrong acceptance; at worst it turns it into another refusal.\n- **Idempotence**: a repeated loop over one pack address is a no-op once the\n  entries are present (registry answers without a link request).\n\n### 4.4 Carried install children (closes R4)\n\nA pack's `:install` row names a manifest address. The lower must refuse the\nchild unless the receiving composition **admits that address for that name**,\nby the same equal-address rule: the composition resolves the name in its own\nfold, asks its admission policy (question = name, address, asserters, as\ntoday), and passes the admitted set to the lower as data\n(`opts {:yin.k/admitted {pym.k :segment/…}}`); an unlisted child is\n`:yin.k/unsatisfied {:yin.k/missing {:yin.k/modules {pym.k <carried address>}}}`\nbefore any attach. Default empty set = fail closed. The copied `:admission`\nrecord from S (`engine.cljc:1436-1443`) is then evidence of S's decision only\nand must be replaced by R's at `link-install` (or dropped).\n\n### 4.5 What the composition must verify, as a checklist\n\n1. Pack address equals the grant's `:checkpoint` (exclusive) or a value it\n   chose itself (fork).\n2. Every key of `:yin.k/modules` was required through its **own** link pair\n   under a DHT or content source it configured; none through `:name-env`\n   seeded from the pack.\n3. Every resulting entry carries `:admission` from **R's** policy or\n   provenance `:composition` chosen by R, and `(:address entry)` equals the pin.\n4. The re-lower is called with the same wire and a `recv` built from the\n   registry value observed in step 3.\n5. The lease was live throughout; a lapse retires the run and the pack is\n   not re-lowered until a fresh attempt.\n\n## 5. What must NOT live in the VM or the lower (D confirmed rejected)\n\n- **No fetch in `resolve-var`.** It would make every `:var` instruction an\n  implicit network operation (invariants: no implicit control flow, callbacks\n  are stream events; I4 no operation waits, `yin.vm.linker.md:179-182`; I6 the\n  VM never invokes a loader, `:196-199`). The custody gate stops link\n  requests under `:exporting` (`module.cljc:541-543`); a resolve-time fetch\n  would have to re-invent that discipline inside the evaluator.\n- **No admission context in the VM.** \"Who is asking\" is composition-owned\n  holder context, never a datum (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md:2248-2255`); the VM\n  has none to give.\n- **No clock, no lease, no budget in the lower.** The lower is pure and\n  portable; liveness is `dao.lease`'s and the drive's (`linker.cljc:1958-1960`).\n- **No name environment or trust in the carrier.** A pack may pin an address\n  (a fact of S's state); it may not assert who may bind a name (a pinned\n  address \"is never a name claim\", `yin.vm.linker.dht.md:1835-1837`).\n- **No registry entries in the row table.** Code travels as segments; a\n  module's installed slice does not travel (continuation §2.5 `:360-366`\n  stands); only the binding fact `name → address` travels.\n- **No language, frontend or session key** anywhere in the new shape\n  (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md:2302-2316`).\n\n## 6. Verdict on Q5\n\n**C**, implemented as B plus one map, in UCF's already-specified shape.\n\n- **A is unacceptable.** It has the only runtime symptom in the gap list\n  (`collab/1793430000000-architect-gap-list.report.md:125`), it fails\n  **after** effects under R's authority (R8), and it hides version skew (R1,\n  R6) completely.\n- **B alone is insufficient.** Refusing before attach fixes R8 but keeps\n  name-only satisfaction, so dependency confusion (R1), skew (R6) and the\n  TOCTOU on name presence (R5) remain, and a fetch loop driven by names\n  gives M1 a free hand to choose what R loads (R2) subject only to R's trust\n  set.\n- **C's format change is justified.** UCF §7.6.4 already requires\n  `:yin.k/modules` keyed by manifest address with equal-address\n  satisfaction (`:1697-1702`) and §7.6.5 already lists it in the outcome\n  (`:1716-1718`); the row carrier dropped it (continuation §2.5). \"Derive,\n  don't persist\" does not apply: the binding S's registry held is a fact of\n  S's state that no query over R's rows can recover. Cost: one map entry per\n  registry-answered name in the task row's `requires` slot; the lift already\n  resolves the entry (`rows.cljc:1616`) and only has to keep its `:address`.\n  Packs re-mint (recorded in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md`); no backward\n  compatibility is owed.\n- **Pin, do not fetch by identity.** By-address link requests are refused by\n  the interpreter (`interpreter.cljc:483-486`) and admission has no asserter\n  for a bare address. Keep the fetch by name through R's fold and admission;\n  use the pin only as the equality the lower and the loop check. This keeps\n  \"A is the only code identity\" intact: the pin is a manifest content address,\n  a claim about S's binding, not a new code identity.\n- **R4 lands in the same slice.** It is the actual injection route in today's\n  code and it is the same check (equal address, R-admitted) applied to\n  carried install children.\n\n## 7. Tests and pins (portable, cljc) and documentation\n\n### 7.1 Tests\n\n| # | Pin | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| T1 | Lift of harness row 17 (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:397-409`, `host.mod/f` through the registry) emits `requires {:yin.k/modules {host.mod <address>}}`; a name answered by `E`, the store or a module store emits no entry. | `rows_test.cljc` |\n| T2 | Lower into a receiver lacking `host.mod` throws `:yin.k/unsatisfied :yin.k/discovery :complete :yin.k/missing {:yin.k/modules {host.mod <address>} :obligations #{host.mod/f}}` with **zero** `attach!` calls (attach-count law). | `rows_test.cljc` |\n| T3 | Lower into a receiver holding `host.mod` at another `:address` throws `:module-pin-mismatch` naming both addresses, zero attaches. Receiver at the equal address lowers. | `rows_test.cljc` |\n| T4 | A `nil` pin (host module) is satisfied only by equal export profiles; a same-named host module with another profile refuses. | `rows_test.cljc` |\n| T5 | A carried `:install` child whose manifest address is not in `opts :yin.k/admitted` refuses before attach; listed, it lowers; after `link-install` at R the entry's `:admission` is R's, not the carried one. | `rows_test.cljc` + `linker_require_test.cljc` |\n| T6 | Loop test with a scripted composition: refusal → one `require` per key → admission medium holds exactly one question per key with R's fold's address → re-lower succeeds from the same bytes. Mutants killed: loop that seeds `:name-env` from the pack (no question appended → test fails); loop that passes `:derivation :trusted` (response `:trust :composition` → test fails). | new `ucf/fetch_loop_test.cljc` |\n| T7 | Scripted-tick lease: a loop whose link never answers lapses `:silence` and retires every run-owned request; no grant was proposed; a second attempt is a fresh run. | same, with `dao.lease` fixtures (`lease_test.cljc` style) |\n| T8 | Cross-host: the refusal map is `=` and re-encodes byte-identically on JVM, Node, Dart (Stage E row). | `stage_e_rows_test.cljc` |\n| T9 | Layering: `yin.vm.engine`, `yin.vm.semantic*`, `yin.vm.ucf.rows` require neither `yin.vm.linker.interpreter` nor `yin.vm.linker.dht` nor `dao.lease` (D stays rejected by construction). | `layering_test.clj` |\n| T10 | `resolve-var` unchanged: a name absent from env/store/prims/registry still fails with `Unable to resolve symbol`; no fetch, no park. | `engine` tests |\n\n### 7.2 Documentation\n\n- **continuation §2.5**: replace \"Linked registry entries do not travel …\n  composition prerequisite\" with: the entry does not travel; the **binding\n  fact** does, as `:yin.k/modules {name manifest-address|nil}` in\n  `requires`; the receiver's registry is kept as given and must satisfy each\n  binding by equal address (host modules by export profile).\n- **continuation §7.1 step 6**: the registry step records the entry's\n  address. **§7.2 dependencies row**: add the module check and the two\n  refusal kinds; delete \"The lower does not re-discharge free names\"\n  (`:1827-1830`) and the §7.1 sentence at `:1700-1701`. **§11 Q5**: closed\n  as C; **Q3**: amend \"remains the linker's responsibility\" to the admitted\n  set passed to the lower.\n- **UCF §7.6.4/7.6.5**: no change in substance; note the row carrier now\n  implements it. **§7.11**: add a blocker row \"Receiver-side module\n  satisfaction and carried-install admission: lower refuses before attach;\n  the fetch loop is the composition's, leased, before the grant proposal;\n  tests T1-T10\". Cross-reference 14.2.3 step 2 for ordering.\n- **yin.vm.linker.dht.md 7.5.11**: add that a lowered pack's install child\n  enters the family only through R's admission.\n- **known-gaps**: one row for R4 (P1 until the slice lands), one for the\n  missing per-run lease (P2, reserved `:lease` policy).\n\nTHREAT-MODEL-READY\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n?? collab/1792130530811-engineer-module-pins.report.md\n?? collab/1792131636522-engineer-module-pins-r2a.report.md\n?? collab/1793430000000-architect-gap-list.report.md\n?? collab/1793520000000-architect-fetch-threat-model.report.md\n?? collab/1793530000000-engineer-module-pins.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793530000000-engineer-module-pins.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793540000000-architect-module-pins-review.fable.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793540000000-architect-module-pins-review.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793550000000-architect-module-pins-review.report.md\n?? collab/1793560000000-engineer-module-pins-r2a.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793560000000-engineer-module-pins-r2a.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793570000000-architect-module-pins-r2a-confirm.fable.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793570000000-architect-module-pins-r2a-confirm.prompt.md\n?? collab/1793580000000-architect-module-pins-r2a-confirm.report.md\n?? collab/1793590000000-engineer-module-pins-r2b.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n?? collab/1793590000000-engineer-module-pins-r2b.prompt.md\nAGENTS.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
